**`if let Ok(Some(..))` hides a corrupt active-version marker.**

`version_manager::active_nu_binary` returns `Err` when `nu_state/active-version.json` exists but cannot be read or parsed (`read_active_version`, `src/nu/version_manager.rs` Lines 33-37). Line 47 discards that error and falls through to the legacy path.

The result: a corrupt or truncated marker makes Numan silently resolve a different Nu binary. `src/cmd/doctor.rs` uses `managed_nu_binary` at Line 461 and Line 1547, so even `numan doctor` reports a healthy-looking path instead of the real fault. That is fake readiness.

The `PathBuf` return type cannot carry the error. Add a fallible variant and use it where a diagnostic matters, or at minimum emit the error to stderr before falling back.





Based on coding guidelines: "Error paths must return `anyhow::Result` with context" and "Treat the lockfile as the authoritative source of truth; derived projections such as autoload state are not authoritative."

